<h2>How the dispute arose</h2>
<p>According to the FIR registered at P.S. Tilak Nagar, the petitioner\'s mother was introduced to the jeweller in October 2023 and established credibility through punctual payments. The relationship then shifted to larger credit purchases for a wedding, with jewellery handed over against approval vouchers and assurances of prompt settlement. When a ₹60 lakh cheque could not be encashed — the account stood debit frozen — and dues of roughly ₹1.48 crore remained unpaid, the complaint followed. The mother was arrested in January 2026 and bailed the next day; the petitioner had earlier been granted anticipatory bail and then sought quashing of the FIR itself under Article 226 and Section 528 BNSS.</p>

<h2>The breach-versus-cheating framework</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>Mere breach of contract</strong><p>Goods supplied, payment defaulted, no material suggesting dishonest intention when the transaction began. The remedy is civil — a recovery suit, summary proceedings, or negotiable-instruments prosecution for a dishonoured cheque.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>Cheating</strong><p>A false representation or assurance made before delivery, which induced the owner to part with property he would otherwise have withheld. Dishonest intention at the inception transforms the default into an offence under Section 318 BNS.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The petitioner\'s argument placed the case firmly in the first column: a commercial relationship, early payments honestly made, a belated FIR, and pressure tactics. The Court\'s answer was that the second column is pleaded on the face of the FIR — inducement preceding delivery — and that sorting genuine inception-stage dishonesty from hindsight reconstruction is precisely what investigation and trial exist to do.</p>

<h2>What the judgment does not decide</h2>
<p>Nothing in the dismissal forecloses the defence. The Court was explicit that its observations bind no one at trial: the evidentiary weight of the early payments, the circumstances of the frozen account, the significance of the five-month gap between complaint and FIR, and the conspiracy allegation under Section 61 BNS all remain open. The petitioner\'s anticipatory bail, granted in January 2026, continues to govern his liberty. The decision operates at the threshold only — the FIR survives because it discloses a cognizable offence, not because the Court found the offence proved.</p>

<h2>Guidance for traders and buyers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Sellers extending large credit should contemporaneously document the assurances on which they rely — messages, vouchers, acknowledgments — because inducement is proved by what was said before delivery, not after default. Buyers facing a criminal complaint over a genuine commercial failure should resist the reflex of an immediate quashing petition where the FIR pleads inception-stage inducement; the stronger course is often to contest the case on evidence while protecting liberty through bail, and to document financial distress that post-dates the transactions.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Delhi High Court quashing practice after the BNSS continues under Section 528, which preserves the inherent powers formerly exercised under Section 482 CrPC, alongside the writ jurisdiction under Article 226. The tests remain those developed under the earlier code.</p>
</div>
